From 50ad45e82082c1aff4ad7dc0e9b64f06a20b1988 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: evilcomputer12 <evilcomputer12@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 00:39:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] vphone-linux: give the IOSurface device the guest-RAM
 memfds (fix black display)

On Linux the library maps and remaps the guest's pages through the memfd
QEMU backs its RAM with (shared views; see platform::vulkan::guest_memory
-> lease_guest_memory). The graphics device already received those files,
but the IOSurface device -- through which the display's descriptor table and
command ring are mapped -- did not, so new_range_mapping's remap found "no
file behind" the guest RAM, the ring and table could not be mapped, and the
display stayed black (the guest's GPU driver then looped "Trying to restart
GPU (Undefined)").

- RvgIOSurfaceDeviceConfig gains a `memory_files` field, one entry per
  range as RvgDeviceConfig's, passed to the shared memory_map() helper. It
  is additive and struct_size-versioned: a host built against the old size
  passes 32 and gets fileless ranges, the previous behaviour.
- aligned_shared_map is memoised by the backing file's identity (its device
  and inode, from fstat) and the offset and length mapped, so the graphics
  device and the IOSurface device share the one 2 MiB-aligned mapping the
  device leases and registers, instead of each making its own at a different
  host address of which only one is registered for remaps. The alignment is
  what an isolated task's whole_pages() needs on a 16 KiB-page guest, since
  QEMU maps the memfd at only host-page (4 KiB) alignment.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
